.\" Man page for agwpe.conf
.\"
.TH AGWPE.CONF 5 "7 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
agwpe.conf \- configuration for ax25netd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agwpe.conf
lists the AGWPE servers ("upstreams") which
.BR ax25netd (8)
connects to and exposes to its local clients.
.PP
This file is read by
.BR ax25netd (8)
alone.  A program served by libax25's userspace shim reaches the joined
radios over the daemon's loop port (see
.BR ax25common.conf (5)),
because the
.BR AXSOCK_HOST " / " AXSOCK_PORT
environment variables, the shim's only choice of server, name the daemon
there by default; point them at one of these servers instead, and the
daemon is skipped.  Either way the program never reads this file.
.PP
Each line defines one upstream:
.PP
.RS
.I name host tcp-port
.RE
.PP
The
.I name
is shown in the reply to the AGWPE "port information" query.  The
.I host
and
.I tcp-port
are where the AGWPE server listens (Direwolf defaults to port 8000).
.PP
The
.I host
accepts IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and host names; IPv6 literals may be
written in the bracketed notation
.B [2001:db8::1]
(brackets are stripped before lookup).  A host beginning with
.B /
names a unix domain socket instead of a TCP port, useful to reach an
AGWPE server on the same machine without TCP; in that case the
.I tcp-port
column is ignored and may be written as
.B \-
or left out:
.PP
.RS
.nf
radio0	/var/run/agwpe/radio.sock	-
.fi
.RE
.PP
The AGWPE port numbers handed out to the local clients are flat and depend
only on the order of the upstreams in this file, never on the channel
layout of the upstreams: upstream \fIi\fR (counting from 0) owns the ports
\fIi*16\fR .. \fIi*16+15\fR and its channel \fIc\fR is port
\fIi*16+c\fR.  The port list is learned from the upstream's reply to the
"port information" query, so the numbering survives an upstream restart
with a changed number of channels.  A single-channel Direwolf keeps its
port number 0.
.PP
The reserved name
.B loop
enables the virtual local loopback upstream (AGWPE port 255).  It needs no
host or tcp-port; a line
.PP
.RS
.B loop
.RE
.PP
or, for readability,
.PP
.RS
.B loop
.I - -
.RE
.PP
is enough.  Local AX.25 services reachable on the loop port must have a
matching "loop" entry in
.BR axports (5).
.PP
An optional line
.PP
.RS
.B auth
.I no|extern|yes
.RE
.PP
selects how much authentication loop port clients need.  The default is
.BR extern :
clients connecting over loopback (127.0.0.1 and ::1) are trusted, while any
other client must log in with credentials from
.BR agwpe_shadow.conf (5)
before it may issue any command.
.B yes
asks every client to log in, loopback included;
.B no
trusts every client.
.B required
and
.B always
are synonyms for
.BR yes ,
.B loop
for
.BR extern .
.PP
The loop port endpoint itself (the unix domain socket, the TCP port and
the socket group) is not configured here but in the shared
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
which
.BR ax25netd (8)
and its local clients read alike.
.PP
An optional line
.PP
.RS
.B autoroute
.I yes|no
.RE
.PP
controls automatic digipeater resolution for connects that name no
digipeaters ("connect port dest", the AGWPE "C" and "c" commands).
When enabled (the default, mirroring the kernel AX.25 stack) the daemon
asks the
.BR ax25rtd (8)
route cache for the destination on the target port and uses the learned
path.  A connect that names its digipeaters (the "v" command) is never
touched, and a connect to the loop port is never resolved.  An absent
or unresponsive route daemon is not an error: the plain connect is sent
instead.
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
	auth	extern
	autoroute	yes
	radio0	localhost	8000
	radio1	192.168.1.20	8000
	radio2	[2001:db8::1]	8000
	radio3	/var/run/agwpe/radio.sock	-
	loop	-	-
.fi
.SH FILES
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/agwpe.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR ax25rtd (8),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR agwpe_shadow.conf (5),
.BR axports (5),
.BR axsock (7)
